00-5cd3-874b-8f3915873fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504493}] :question/id #uuid "dbe20736-69a2-5914-aaf4-587bd8fc1776" :question/math-academy-id "q-106848" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f0d8e4c-11e0-585b-98a8-e1e65e946a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556531} {:answer/id #uuid "360d0293-cec9-58b0-9fa0-f3f513479858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556547} {:answer/id #uuid "840aef6c-7174-5636-bec4-0b4c79dba19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556740} {:answer/id #uuid "c713eb9d-b36e-5847-8500-ce5007937f65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556908} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c1dda6-8000-59cb-8a0d-65f0284741cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556547} :answer-field/id #uuid "76fb5c7d-850b-5730-b4f9-b378b453ae17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557134}] :question/id #uuid "e07c46c7-d38b-5617-a5f3-42fc8a91e6b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75098" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119607 :question/id #uuid "50685ac4-9980-5841-a669-783f6086a9dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4770" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/c2/c21c2b99cdd8480ab891a23f92687d29230da7da9de9bd75a903919e54c48a1f.svg)\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_2E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd1da0c-9706-54e7-a5f2-f3d28e865dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300194} {:answer/id #uuid "412e17c7-0020-53f1-a0ef-539e1365d3d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300281} {:answer/id #uuid "4b04aaca-0605-5891-9aea-47020aa5f0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300348} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7cfc9b-c118-57b5-980b-fd9d456c0e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300566} {:answer/id #uuid "a9cb8b03-0892-5abc-894c-fd8f3ea0915c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/valu